    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Over the past few years, cars have become smarter and smarter. Some of them can even drive themselves. Now, our roads have followed suit. In the United Kingdom alone, there are around 650 kilometres of “smart roads” in use. Across the world, many more are being built. Some new technologies are being put to use."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h1",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Roads that "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "recharge",
            "zh": "给……充电"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Global warming has forced us to search for new energy sources. Why not look to the roads? With the right technologies, cars around the world could be producing energy as they go."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Piezoelectric roads mix traditional asphalt with new materials. They use "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "vibrations",
            "zh": "震动"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " from passing cars to produce electricity. Piezoelectricity, in fact, is nothing new. It was invented in 1880. However, it has never been widely used before now. The U.K.’s Lancaster University is going to change that. It has been improving the technology, including developing roadside batteries that can store electricity."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h2",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Built-in brain"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Roads of the future may be able to know how many people are on them. They could send them updates on weather and traffic jams, and recharge electric cars as they drive."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "This seems to be a dream, but one American company has almost made it come true. By "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "sensing",
            "zh": "检测出"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the weight of a vehicle, the company’s smart roads can track the speed of each wheel. “We are basically making very large touchpads,” says Tim Sylvester, CEO of the company. “But instead of looking for fingers, our roads look for tires.” After having collected traffic data, the roads will send it to the drivers."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h3",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Roads that never freeze"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "It can be dangerous to drive in snowy weather. A tech company in "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Israel",
            "zh": "以色列"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " has developed the Snowless roads. When they detect snow or ice on their surfaces, the roads will heat themselves, melting the snow or ice away. The project has been successfully tested on a few Canadian roads and in some of its car parks."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
